@meta
id: 01-05
title: Leaving without the colon
chapter: 01
type: lesson
skills: cmdline, insert-a, esc, survival-motion
par: 20
minutes: 3
vscode: Ctrl-S then Ctrl-W
open: standup.md

@brief
There is a shorter way out. From normal mode, `ZZ` — capital Z twice —
writes the file if it changed and quits. No colon, no Enter, two keys of
the same finger.

It is the same thing as `:wq` in almost every case, and it is what a lot
of people's hands end up doing. The difference is that `ZZ` only writes
when there is something to write, so opening a file to read it and
pressing `ZZ` leaves the timestamp alone.

The Blocked line in standup.md is cut short. It should read:

    Blocked: waiting on staging access

Finish the line and leave with `ZZ` instead of `:wq`.

@keys
| `ZZ` | write if changed, then quit |
| `ZQ` | quit and throw the changes away |
| `$` | last character of the line |
| `a` | append to the right of the cursor |

@hints
It is the last line of the file. `j` four times gets you there; `$` gets
you to the end of it.
--
You are adding to the right of the final character, so `a`, not `i`.
--
Type the missing word, `Esc`, then two capital Zs. Hold Shift and press
z twice.

@solution
    j j j j $ a <space> access <Esc> ZZ

`ZZ` has a twin, `ZQ`, which quits and discards. The pair maps onto `:wq`
and `:q!`. Learn `ZZ` because your hands will use it; know `ZQ` exists
because one day you will want it.

Be careful with the capital. `zz` in lower case is a real command too — it
redraws the screen with the cursor line in the middle — so a missed Shift
does something harmless but confusing rather than nothing at all. If the
file did not close, that is probably what happened.

@start standup.md
Daily notes for Wednesday standup

Yesterday: reviewed the refund window PR
Today: pairing on the payment retry
Blocked: waiting on staging

